1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,260
Your phone slips out of your hand on

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:05,780
a balcony twelve floors up, and it stops.

3
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,580
Not in your hand.

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,040
In the air, a metre below the railing,

5
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:11,420
screen still glowing.

6
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:14,140
A pigeon hangs over the street with its

7
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:15,280
wings half open.

8
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:17,660
The smoke from a chimney is a grey

9
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:18,260
sculpture.

10
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,440
The cars below are parked in the middle

11
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:22,180
of the road.

12
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,700
And there is no sound at all.

13
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:27,140
You reach down, take your phone out of

14
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:28,500
the air, and count.

15
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:29,560
One.

16
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:30,620
Two.

17
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:31,680
Three.

18
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:32,740
Four.

19
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:33,800
Five.

20
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:34,680
And the city starts again,

21
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:36,580
like someone pressed play.

22
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:40,160
Overnight, you got the power of The World,

23
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:43,720
the time-stopping ability from JoJo's Bizarre Adventure.

24
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:45,560
Not Dio's mansion.

25
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:46,700
Not his knives.

26
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:50,520
Just this: you can stop time for everyone

27
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:51,200
except you.

28
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:53,360
In the story, it starts at about five

29
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:53,700
seconds.

30
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:56,060
Over the next forty days, you're going to

31
00:00:56,060 --> 00:00:58,460
learn what five seconds is really worth.

32
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:00,120
Breakfast.

33
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,840
Your elbow knocks a glass of orange juice

34
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:03,560
off the table.

35
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:04,960
You don't even think.

36
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:06,580
Everything stops.

37
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:08,860
The juice hangs in the air like an

38
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:11,120
orange ribbon, half in the glass, half out.

39
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:12,760
You walk around it.

40
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:16,140
You poke it, and it's oddly stiff, like

41
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:16,520
jelly.

42
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:18,760
You put the glass back under it, and

43
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:20,740
when time starts, the juice falls right back

44
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:20,980
in.

45
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:22,560
You try again.

46
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:23,720
And again.

47
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:26,180
You count every time, and it's always the

48
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:26,440
same:

49
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:28,880
five seconds, then the world moves.

50
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:30,060
Rule one:

51
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:31,900
you get five seconds.

52
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:32,980
Not six.

53
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:34,860
Not "a bit longer if you really need

54
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:35,160
it".

55
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:35,800
Five.

56
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:39,220
On day two you stop time in the

57
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,180
middle of a busy street, and the first

58
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,280
thing that hits you isn't the frozen people.

59
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:44,800
It's the silence.

60
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:47,460
Sound is just air bumping into air.

61
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:50,100
In normal life it moves at about 343

62
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,200
metres per second, passed from molecule to molecule.

63
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,960
The molecules themselves are flying around at about

64
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,300
500 metres per second.

65
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:00,000
In your five seconds, none of them move.

66
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,020
So nothing reaches your ears.

67
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:04,840
Not the guitar, not the dog, not your

68
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:05,360
own footsteps.

69
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:07,000
You clap your hands.

70
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:07,860
Nothing.

71
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:08,940
You shout.

72
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:09,900
Nothing.

73
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:12,780
It's the quietest place you have ever been,

74
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:14,680
and it's in the middle of a city.

75
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:15,640
Rule two:

76
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:17,500
frozen time is silent.

77
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:19,800
If you want to talk to someone, you

78
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,220
have to wait.

79
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,780
On day four you test the strangest part.

80
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:26,240
You stop time in a park, move a

81
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,580
frisbee two metres to the left, and let

82
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:28,860
go.

83
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,920
The dog jumps for where the frisbee was.

84
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,400
Then it looks around, confused.

85
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:36,660
But nobody else even blinks.

86
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,500
That's because a human needs about a quarter

87
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,600
of a second just to react to something

88
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:42,120
they see.

89
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:44,780
Five seconds is about twenty of those.

90
00:02:44,780 --> 00:02:47,140
You can do a lot in twenty reactions,

91
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:49,640
and to everyone else it looks like one.

92
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,380
Rule three: from the outside, there's no gap.

93
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:54,920
Things just jump.

94
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,900
On day seven you go to a running

95
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:00,320
track to find out how far five seconds

96
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,080
can take you.

97
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:05,000
The fastest man ever, Usain Bolt, ran 100

98
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,120
metres in 9.58 seconds.

99
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,540
That's an average of about ten metres per

100
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:09,880
second.

101
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,640
In five seconds, even he would only get

102
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:13,620
about fifty metres.

103
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,500
You are not Usain Bolt.

104
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,360
You stop time, run as hard as you

105
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,600
can, and when it restarts you're a lot

106
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,020
less than fifty metres away, out of breath,

107
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:24,540
with your shoelace untied.

108
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:25,640
Rule four:

109
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:28,300
stopping time doesn't make you faster.

110
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,600
It only makes everyone else slower.

111
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:33,960
On day eleven you film yourself.

112
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:36,900
Phone on a tripod, you stop time,

113
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,760
walk across the room, and let go.

114
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:41,820
On the video, you are on the left,

115
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:44,080
and then, in a single frame, you are

116
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:44,640
on the right.

117
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:45,720
No blur.

118
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:47,200
No in-between.

119
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,240
You just teleport.

120
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:50,900
And here's a real thing that sounds made

121
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,360
up: in 2013,

122
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:56,160
physicists in Darmstadt, in Germany, managed to hold

123
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:58,400
light still inside a crystal for up to

124
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,500
a minute, and store an image in it.

125
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:02,320
So the light around you in your frozen

126
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:03,640
world isn't impossible.

127
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:05,940
It's just very, very hard.

128
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:09,800
Rule five: cameras never see the frozen part.

129
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:11,120
They only see the jump.

130
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,760
Someone else filmed it too.

131
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,020
On day eighteen, a video called "Glitch Man"

132
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:19,020
has nine million views.

133
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,960
A person on a pedestrian crossing is on

134
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:23,580
one side of a bus, and in the

135
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:25,080
next frame they're on the other side.

136
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:27,400
Half the comments say it's editing.

137
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:30,260
The other half say it's a time traveller.

138
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:32,920
Nobody says "time stopper", because that's not a

139
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:33,380
real thing.

140
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:36,940
And you notice what everyone notices first: how

141
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:38,820
easy it would be to take things.

142
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:39,920
A wallet.

143
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:41,420
An exam answer.

144
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:43,480
A card at a casino table.

145
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:45,240
You walk past the casino.

146
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,120
Rule six: if it isn't yours in normal

147
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:50,640
time, it isn't yours in frozen time.

148
00:04:51,660 --> 00:04:54,140
On day twenty-five, two people in grey suits

149
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:54,720
find you.

150
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:55,540
Very polite.

151
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:58,660
They have a room full of stopwatches and

152
00:04:58,660 --> 00:05:00,720
a laser beam you're supposed to cross without

153
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:01,060
breaking.

154
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,540
You stop time, step through the beam, and

155
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:05,460
stand on the other side.

156
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:07,620
To them, you just appear.

157
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:10,580
They do it again and again, all afternoon.

158
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:13,900
And something happens that you didn't expect: the

159
00:05:13,900 --> 00:05:16,720
more you practise, the longer it gets.

160
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:18,080
Five seconds.

161
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:19,160
Six.

162
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:20,420
Seven.

163
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:23,200
In the story, Dio pushes his time stop

164
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:25,880
to about nine seconds, according to the Wikipedia

165
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:26,320
summary.

166
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:29,500
On day thirty-nine, in a white room, with

167
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:30,440
a woman holding a clipboard,

168
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:31,940
you reach nine.

169
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,040
She writes one line in her report:

170
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,340
"Duration: increasing."

171
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:40,140
On day forty, you're walking home when an

172
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:41,620
apartment block catches fire.

173
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,160
A family is stuck on a fourth-floor balcony.

174
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:46,100
The fire escape is gone.

175
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:47,640
You stop time.

176
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,020
The flames look like orange glass.

177
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:52,520
The smoke is grey cotton you can push

178
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:53,640
aside with your hand.

179
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:55,920
It's the most beautiful thing you have ever

180
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,940
seen, and you only have nine seconds.

181
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:00,240
You get up the stairs inside the building

182
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:00,800
in four.

183
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:02,580
You pick up the youngest child.

184
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:04,020
Two seconds to the window.

185
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:05,120
Three to the ground.

186
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:06,260
Time starts.

187
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,520
The crowd sees a child appear on the

188
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:09,560
pavement and screams.

189
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:10,800
You wait.

190
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:12,140
You catch your breath.

191
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:13,360
You stop it again.

192
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:16,020
Nine seconds at a time, you bring down

193
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:17,900
the whole family, one by one.

194
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:20,320
To everyone outside, it takes less than a

195
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:20,640
minute.

196
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:23,880
For you, it takes much longer, and every

197
00:06:23,880 --> 00:06:26,100
one of those seconds is silent, and yours

198
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:26,440
alone.

199
00:06:26,440 --> 00:06:29,660
This is the part nobody warns you about.

200
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:32,380
Every time you stop the world, you keep

201
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:32,760
living.

202
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:35,940
Your heart beats, your hair grows, you get

203
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:36,540
a little older.

204
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:38,400
The rest of the world doesn't.

205
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,520
Over forty days you've lived hours that nobody

206
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:42,080
else lived.

207
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,880
And you spent all of them alone,

208
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:46,620
in a silent city where nobody could answer

209
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:46,860
you.

210
00:06:47,260 --> 00:06:48,620
The next day you meet a friend for

211
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:48,960
coffee.

212
00:06:49,780 --> 00:06:51,700
Halfway through a story, they start to laugh,

213
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:53,680
the big kind of laugh where they can't

214
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:54,320
finish the sentence.

215
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,660
And you realise you could freeze this.

216
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,040
You could walk around it and look at

217
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:00,060
it for nine seconds.

218
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:01,580
You don't.

219
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:02,460
Rule seven:

220
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:05,020
the moments worth keeping are the ones you

221
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:05,360
share.

222
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:07,360
You can't share a moment you've stopped.

223
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:10,340
So: if you woke up tomorrow able to

224
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:13,140
stop time for five seconds, what's the first

225
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:13,900
thing you'd do with it?

226
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:16,440
And what's the one moment you'd never stop?

227
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:17,920
Tell us in the comments.
